n word there was an extraordinary  esperen sea of communication at war  books all the ingoing and outgoing  correspondence was pulled everybody  could have read it and everybody was  expected to read it  there was little reliance on the  telephone if the telephone was used a  memorandum had to be written and they  had a rule of four in which any  transaction had to be signed off by four  directors of the company all of this is  to me fascinating and more important  than let us say the sex life of Marilyn  Monroe it's important in two ways it's  important because it tells us how the  financial world changed in the  transition from the culture that had  proved produced the depression to the  culture that produced the glory years of  the post-war economy and it's important  also because it tells us what we're lost  in the generation after Warburg and his  contemporaries faded from the scene he  died in 1982 I calculated that the  average duration of the career of a  chief executive of a major American Bank  in 2007 on the eve of the crisis was 25  years they have basically all come in to  the financial world the year that sea  when Warburg died where did it come from  where did that sense of an ethical  framework come from what can we do to  produce that kind of framework in the  next generation of finances partly it  came from experienced ladies and  gentlemen if you have lived through  hyperinflation depression and the rise  of Hitler you would probably be quite  risk-averse but it wasn't just that it  was also the way he was brought up to me  the most moving document that I read  when I was writing this book was a  document in which Warburg remembered  what his mother had said to him on the  eve of his Bar Mitzvah I want to quote  it to you and I want you to memorize it  and I want you to say it to your  children and your grandchildren from now  on my dear boy you must pray alone in  the evening and you must always ask  yourself before you pray what mistakes  you have made during the preceding day  or what you could have done better if a  whole number of mistakes or omissions do  not at once occur to you then you must  look deeper into yourself until you have  attained the necessary self-knowledge we  all make many mistakes every day and the  most important thing is to be critical  of your own mistakes in the most  unsparing way that is the only way to  arrive at honest prayer Simon Warburg  grew up believing that you should always  cry over spilt milk and you should  always cross bridges before you came to  them I think that's because of his  mother and perhaps if today's bankers  had had mothers like Lucy  Warburg we might not be in quite that  economic mess that we're in now with  that I'm going to thank you for your  attention and invite you to ask me  difficult questions  the rules of the game are as follows  someone other than me will choose who  asks the questions you just have to  raise your hands if you are chosen a  microphone will magically appear in your  hand when the microphone appears in your  hand please stand up so that c-span and  a waiting nation can see who you are  and then when you begin to speak  remember the object of the exercise is  to ask a question not to make a speech  or give a lecture that's me  I do that so questions please question  what was his effects on his children I I  learnt a lot from writing this book over  12 years as a father and one of the  things I learned which is very important  is that when you have a son you have not  cloned yourself many men make the  mistake of thinking that their son  particularly their firstborn son is a  clone and they expect that some to be  the same as them and I think Sigmund War  would make that made that mistake I  don't say that he wasn't a loving father  but his expectations of his son George I  think ultimately were not realistic and  didn't take account of his son's  character and temperament and at the  heart of the book there is I think but a  tr